hing that we performed  was to examine that one experiment we  performed was to examine whether  bacterial diversity within the mecfs  patient cohort  and a healthy cohort differed on the  y-axis is phylogenetic diversity how  many species are owed --use do are we  finding in the patients versus controls  as the number of sequences seek obtained  per sample increases this is called a  rarefaction analysis we can see that the  patients and controls differ in their  amount of species detected if you look  very early and you don't have very many  sequences you wouldn't see this  difference between the patients and  controls it's necessary to obtain enough  sequences that one can then begin to see  that there is in fact a loss of species  richness in mecfs microbiomes I'd like  to mention that after this paper was  published a microbiologist at Liberty  University Allen Gillan contacted me and  asked have you compared the micro biomes  of the individuals who reported  gastrointestinal disturbances to  patients who did not report  gastrointestinal disturbance and we'd  actually not done that analysis  so Tony Walters a ruthless lab kindly  checked on this and he found indeed that  the people who reported gastrointestinal  discomfort had less diversity than the  patients who did not report the  gastrointestinal disturbances so this is  an additional piece of information  indicating this abnormality could be  important we also discovered that we had  24 families in general that were  differentially abundant between mecfs  patients and healthy individuals this  these differences are statistically  significant so on this diagram here in  this red box are all of the group's  taxonomic groups that are less abundant  in the patients than in the controls  some of these ones that are back ot use  and species and  families in general are actually ones  that are known to be beneficial for  example members of the room in a Cox a/c  were significantly higher in healthy  individuals than in mecfs patients and  as some members of the Roma cot room in  a caucus a/c are known to produce an  anti-inflammatory protein they're also  known to produce butyrate which is an  anti-inflammatory fatty acid lower  levels of these butyrate producing  bacteria and reiterated South are also  seen in Crohn's disease and ulcerative  colitis  we then investigated whether we could  use the data to make a test for disease  status whether we could identify  patients versus controls using a  combination of the levels of LPS LBP and  s soluble cd14 in the blood with the  microbiome composition in the in our  patient cohort 55% were diagnosed by the  MD as having any CFS and 45% were  healthy in the case of the mecfs we are  actually able to identify 53% of that  55% as a me/cfs  unfortunately however we also identified  12 percent of the normal individuals as  having any CFS according to this test  nevertheless we were able to identify  correctly 30 percent of the normal  individuals what this means is that we  were actually able to identify correctly  83% of the cohort as having either any  CFS or not merely from three blood  levels of these inflammatory proteins  and the microbiome composition so this  is very promising and certainly  indicates that there is a biological  disruption in the microbiome composition  in any CFS with some additional markers  that may be possible to use this kind of  data to distinguish patients and  trolls there are some limitations to  bacterial microbiome analyses however  the data is inadequate to reveal whether  or not a particular bacterial strain is  differentially present in patients  versus healthy individuals for example  some of the patients could have a bad  type of bacterium a variant a strain of  a bacterium that we are only able to  identify that it belongs to a certain  species but we don't know if that's a  bad strain an example is e coli we would  know whether a coli is present in  patients gut microbiome but if it is the  pathogenic type of e coli that has  caused outbreaks of E coli related  disease we would not see that from this  type of analysis other type of analyses  would be needed to identify whether  there is any of that bad e coli present  these bacterial microbiome studies also  don't reveal what eukaryotic pathogens  or beneficial species might be present  such as the yeast and parasites we are  continuing on to analyze our data using  18's sequencing to study what what types  of eukaryotic species may be present we  this data also doesn't indicate where  their viruses are differentially present  and that is another type of analysis  that we are currently carrying out in  conclusion I have shown you that there  is less bacterial diversity in patients  compared to healthy populations there's  the association of abundance of  particular bacterial groups with mecfs  or with healthy status 83% of the  samples can be correctly classified and  anti-inflammatory bacterial species are  reduced in mecfs patients I'd like to  end by just describing briefly a case  report that is now impressed that was  done in collaboration with Betsy Keller  and exercise physiologist  at Ithaca College she had two  identical twins come to her for exercise  testing 4c pets and we thought this was  an opportunity to examine the gut  microbiome of two individuals whose  genotype is quite similar these were two  male twins age 34 one of them would had  mecfs for three and a half years at the  time of testing while the other one was  well in the first seep at the ill twin  had lower maximum oxygen consumption  than the well twin this indicates that  as you might expect the ill twin was  less physically fit than the well twin  likely not having been able to do much  exercise in the last three and a half  years but what was interesting and  relevant to what I described earlier  about exertion intolerance the ill twin  did not reproduce his results in the  first test when he did at the second  time after inducing post exertional  malaise the ill twin reached anaerobic  threshold at 3:13 percent lower oxygen  consumption so this again is that  abnormality of post exertional malaise  that's required for the diagnosis of  mecfs and the new definition the ill  twin had reduced gut microbiome  diversity very similar to what we found  when we were examining the larger cohort  and on the graph on the right what  you're seeing in these bar graphs is  each color represents a different  bacterial group and you can also see  that despite having very similar  genotype as you would expect for  identical twins there's quite a  difference in their bat bacterial  microbiome composition also similar to  the larger study so there is some  additional information about the our  mecfs microbiome study available on a  webinar that I gave last week this is a  patient focused webinar in contrast to  this one which  is really researcher and medical  professional focused I also gave a  patient focused presentation at the end  invest in any conference in London in  June and it's possible to get a DVD of  an entire conference at the website  indicated below we will also be  presenting some additional information  about the gut microbiome at the upcoming  ia CFS and II conference this is the  major scientific conference held on this  disease and it will be in Fort  Lauderdale in October and the lead  author on the bacterial microbiome study  will give some new information about the  viral microbiome and graduate student  Alexandre Mandarin Oh will give a poster  about eukaryotes in the any CFS gut  microbiome and thank you for your  attention  Thank You Maureen for that informative  presentation now time for Q&amp;A if you  have a question you'd like to ask miss  Hanson please do so now just click on  the green Q&amp;A button at the lower left  of the presentation window and type your  question into the box that appears on  your screen  then click the send button we will  answer as many of your questions as we  have time for all right let's get  started our first question is marine is  there a genetic basis as to why some  individuals would have acquired a less  diverse microbiome or is it's thought to  be simply the car genetic effects on the  microbiome that are known there have  been studies in fact there's a very  interesting twin study being done by my  colleague Ruth ley to look at the  genetic effects on the gut microbiome  however in the case of mecfs we don't  know much about what effect the genetics  might have on the this difference in the  gut microbiome certainly in the case of  the twins who have largely the same  genome they have differences in the gut  microbiome despite having been raised in  the same household been exposed to the  same microbes initially my suspicion is  that the reason that's different is the  result of the ill twins alteration  resulting from his his mecfs status but  it would be very interesting to examine  the genotypes further next question  romina seats microbe I believe is  abundant in ruminants it is a vegetarian  diet better for mecfs management  know much about weather weather diet  okay we don't know how their diet is  actually helpful in mecfs patients  anecdotally report that certain diets  are helpful to them but there really  haven't been adequate studies to  conclude whether a vegetarian or  non-vegetarian diet is best for mecfs  patients next question has there been a  name Segal transplant studies and mecfs  patients there have there have been a  few anecdotal reports are very small  studies in which individuals have had  fecal transplants and there's at least  one report in a very small group in  which the patients reported improvement  after people transplants but there  haven't been large enough studies to  conclude whether or not this would be  helpful with the symptoms after  publishing our microbiome study I did  have several individuals privately email  me and say that they had had people  transplants and that it helped their  symptoms one individual in fact  indicated that he'd had one and it  helped for several years but then he had  relapsed and there was no you know he  relapsed back to his earlier state so we  really don't know if that if fecal  transplants would help it is something  that would be worth investigating next  question what are the exact or the  static intolerance from sums of immune  CSF  the aesthetic and tolerant symptoms are  usually seen as a problem when  individuals are upright they individuals  seem to be unable to control their  autonomic nervous system properly and  this is actually one of the more  disabling symptoms of the disease it  required results in some individuals  having to be prone or at least reclining  for most of the day because they're  unable to be upright this has been also  objectively demonstrated using a tilt  table test that many mecfs patients can  have an objective measurement of pots  postural orthostatic tachycardia  syndrome and other abnormalities upon  tilt table testing so this this is a  very common symptom and that's one  reason that it's required either  orthostatic intolerance or cognitive  difficulties we're having some great  questions coming in next question do you  think the actuarial microbiome analysis  result is the result or the cause of  mecfs  okay I can only speculate since I have  no evidence either way but if I were to  speculate it would be my opinion that  it's likely a consequence rather than a  cause of the disease however I do think  it is very likely a cause of some of the  symptoms I believe it could be  exacerbating some of the inflammatory  symptoms that people experience the  flu-like feeling some of the other  symptoms that the patients report so  even though I doubt that it's the cause  and I think it is a consequence that  doesn't mean that it wouldn't benefit  the patients to have some help with a  symptom improvement in the gut  microbiome could potentially improve  some of the other symptoms so because we  don't have a treatment for the disease  that is applicable to all patients just  like the people with headache sometimes  headache medication makes them more  comfortable I think if we could improve  the gut microbiome that could also  result in a benefit to the patients now  Maureen this question refers to the  twins that you spoke about in your  presentation did they and do they  currently live apart and if so would  they have developed a different gut  microbiomes as a result  these twins are currently living apart  and potentially some of the differences  in their gut microbiomes could be due to  the fact that they are living apart and  but the fact is that that a lot of your  gut microbiome is actually inherited  from your mother there have been a  number of studies showing that so it's  impossible to know since we don't have  their gut microbiome  available from before the the one of  them became ill we can't know for sure  so some of the difference could be due  to their living apart and having  slightly different diets on the other  hand the fact that the differences were  very consistent with the differences we  saw between the mecfs patients and the  controls makes me suspect that it's  likely that a lot of the differences are  due to the health status of the ill twin  next question  could the modulation of microbiome be a  solution for the illness do you have any  experience in that  okay  with regard with regard to improvement  in any CFS microbiomes  we don't really know how to do that  there have been some reports again often  small reports of improvement when people  took various interventions probiotics  and there have been anecdotal reports  that they're helpful but this is again  something that needs to be studied we  really don't know exactly how to improve  the microbiome of these individuals  whether one might improve it with diet  probiotics people transplants any of  those things are worth studying to find  out if we could improve the gut  microbiome of the patients  Cameron that's a two-part question first  question were the healthy individuals  who reported to have abdominal pain  included as controls in the study and  was there a difference in the diet  microbiome  between these individuals and the other  healthy individuals that's an  interesting question  just like the the fact that we needed to  go back and analyze the the patients who  had worse intestinal symptoms than the  others  we actually haven't gone back to analyze  whether the small number of healthy  individuals who reported there that they  had some intestinal discomfort we  actually haven't done that and it is  something that would be interesting to  do to see if they have worse you know  unusual microbiomes than the other  healthy individuals that would be  interesting to do but we actually have  not done that analysis we did include we  didn't discriminate against the  individuals who were otherwise healthy  but reported that they had some sort of  abdominal discomfort not necessarily not  necessarily pain some of these  individuals may have reported that they  had diarrhea or constipation frequently  so we didn't we given that we had a  fairly small cohort we we didn't  eliminate those individuals from the  analysis  okay we have time for one more question  let's see  Ryan were the CFS patients taking  medications and/or supplements which  might account for the differences in  their microbiome compared to controls we  did survey the patients and controls  before incorporating them into our study  the patients and controls were excluded  if they had taken antibiotics within  three months of the collection of the  fecal samples it was really not feasible  to exclude individuals who might have  been taking some supplements we did we  did you know questioned them about their  supplements but one issue about  controlling for supplements is that for  example we don't know if some of them  you know 